+# v0.6 Update Notes
+
+The Part pages on this wiki mirror the **V5** rulebook revision. The Essence System v0.6 Google
+Doc (September 2026) reorganized the book and changed several rules that the Foundry system now
+implements. Until the Part pages are rewritten, this page carries the v0.6 changes that a player
+or GM most needs, and notes which older pages they supersede. The Foundry system's in-world
+**Player & GM Guide** journal (system version 0.13.0 and later) carries the same text.
+
+## Team Tier and economic scale
+
+Supersedes the personal Tier wording in Part II and Part VIII.
+
+- Team Tier (1 to 5) belongs to the **Team record**, not to a character. It is lasting Campaign
+  progression and only increases, when the GM judges the Campaign ready for a broader scope.
+  There is no point threshold or checklist.
+- When Team Tier increases, every character's remaining Temporary Influence is cleared. Nothing
+  else is refunded or reset: spent Influence, Core consequences, Reach pressure, dice, cards and
+  equipment capacity all follow their own rules.
+- Team Tier is not a rating of a character's competence or of an enemy's strength.
+- Ordinary acquisition: a character can obtain a Chassis or Fitting whose Component Tier is no
+  higher than Team Tier (each Component checked separately, up to Tier 5) when a supplier exists.
+  Personal Reach absorbs the Influence pressure of a significant acquisition; it does not open
+  higher-Tier suppliers. Earned or found equipment above the Team's access is usable and keeps
+  no rent.
+- Judging scale, as reference anchors for one noticeable expense: Tier 1 about $100, Tier 2
+  $1,000, Tier 3 $10,000, Tier 4 $100,000, Tier 5 $1,000,000. Routine expenses below the scale
+  create no pressure; one near the anchor is 1 ordinary pressure; heavier commitments may be
+  several. Printed Influence costs keep their stated amounts at every Tier.
+- The Team record tracks Things and Commitments (tagged Asset, Obligation, Borrowed, Pledged)
+  and Organizations (Friendly, Neutral, Hostile) that matter at the current scale.
+
+## Practical Attribute benefits
+
+New in v0.6; no earlier page covers these.
+
+- **Might**: carry 50 lb of exceptional cargo per point beyond personal equipment, with actual
+  support and hands. Hauling doubles Movement cost.
+- **Grace**: running horizontal jump up to Grace units, with 2 units of run-up and a landing.
+- **Vigor**: hold breath or sustain extreme effort for Vigor minutes (6 x Vigor Rounds) before
+  the hazard procedure; Fatigued is the default strain Condition.
+- **Intellect**: 1 Non-Combat Skill Point per permanent point (creation maximum Rank 2,
+  advancement maximum Rank 5).
+- **Acuity**: one primary language plus Acuity additional languages.
+- **Resolve**: sustain demanding attention for Resolve hours, then check each further hour;
+  failure normally causes Fatigued.
+- **Presence**: dependable allies as Connections, up to permanent Presence.
+- **Adaptability**: once between Recoveries, after an Exploration task fails, reroll up to
+  Adaptability dice together.
+- **Anima**: at each Recovery, after every Resource regains 25% rounded up, distribute Anima
+  extra points among Stamina, Focus and Mana.
+
+Temporary Attribute changes never grant Skill Points, languages or Connections.
+
+## Recovery
+
+Supersedes the Recovery sections of Part III and Part VII.
+
+- A Recovery is a meaningful opportunity the GM recognizes (a secure camp, real treatment,
+  friendly territory). One fictional opportunity gives one Recovery.
+- Each Resource regains 25% of its maximum rounded up. Fatigued halves each base amount and
+  rounds up again, even if the Recovery removes Fatigued. Then distribute Anima extra points
+  wherever capacity remains; points that cannot be placed are lost.
+- Core Wounds recover by their Wound Cards, any number together and in any order: Light Wounds
+  usually with care and one Recovery, Serious Wounds with post-Adventure Downtime rest and
+  healing, Critical Wounds normally after four weeks (twenty-eight qualifying days) of
+  appropriate Downtime care. This is the current playtest baseline.
+- A Recovery reduces the Death Track by 1 for a living character who is not Dying, clears
+  Psionic Strain, removes 1 Manifestation Wound from the shared Calling track, and restores the
+  Adaptability reroll. It does not restore Temporary Wounds, Temporary Influence, Core Influence,
+  Reach pressure or Consumable Kits.
+
+## When an Adventure ends
+
+Supersedes the Adventure sections of Part III.
+
+An Adventure ends when its central undertaking is resolved, abandoned, made impossible or
+transformed; complete success is one ending among several. Ending an Adventure releases only what
+is measured across it: Inventory allocations (choose a new Inventory at the next Armory access),
+ordinary Reach pressure, Temporary Influence whose grant has expired, and Uses whose own rule
+says "at the end of the Adventure". It does not restore Stamina, Focus or Mana, heal Core Wounds,
+erase Core Influence, refill Consumable Kits or repair lost equipment. Downtime then has no fixed
+length and may hold one Recovery or several.
+
+## Enemy construction
+
+Supersedes the Minion/Standard/Elite/Nemesis role tags and the Role Budget material in Part IV
+and Part X.
+
+- Enemies have a **Grade**: Mook, Normal or Elite. Wound capacity is Mook 2, Normal 4, Elite 5.
+  All three are Defeated when it fills; enemies use no severity spaces, Wound Cards or Death
+  Track. A Defeated enemy is unconscious and dying unless Stabilize or a nonlethal hit made it
+  stable.
+- **Mooks and Normals** run a reduced engine: no dice Pools and no Resources. Initiative is a
+  plain 2d10 roll. Mooks have 1 Action per Turn and 4 Task Dice; Normals have 2 Actions and 5
+  Task Dice. A listed native Reaction is usable once per Round, refreshed at the start of each
+  Round. Abilities roll their printed dice against a named Defense with the normal Success Die
+  and Surges. Dazed's burn and the unaware surcharge are waived. One utility Basic Action per
+  Turn (Dash, Reconfigure, Stabilize or Perform Task) uses an Action and costs no dice. Basic
+  Melee Attack, Basic Ranged Attack, Defend and Prepare Action need explicit permission.
+- **Elites** use normal Pools, printed card costs and any printed Roll Limit; Task Dice fall
+  back to the Roll Limit.
+- Limited-use abilities ("X between Recoveries") refresh only after a genuine Recovery, never
+  because a Combat ended or restarted.
+- Default Resilience in the Starting Enemy Examples: Mooks 0, Normals 1 to 2, Elites 2 to 3; no
+  Temporary Wounds. The ten examples are in the Foundry system's **Starting Enemy Examples**
+  compendium.
+- Encounter categories by target length: Minor 1 to 2 Rounds, Severe 2 to 3, Critical 3 to 5,
+  Boss usually 3 to 5 with a Leader or Solo structure. A short Adventure holds about 1 to 2
+  meaningful combats, a medium one 2 to 4, a long one 4 to 6 or more.
+
+## Other v0.6 changes already in the Foundry system
+
+- Base Action and Reaction Pools are 6 dice; Initiative commits 1 die or more (no 0-die pass).
+- Advancement Points replace Level.
+- Non-combat rolls are made against a Difficulty from 1 to 10.
+- Damage is applied by printed component and type; a flat reduction applies once to the total.
+- Death is final. Stabilize is a Basic Action (burn 3). Prepare Action is a Basic Action (burn 2
+  and reserve dice).
+- Combat Style Specialties follow Part X: Threads (duplicates allowed), Authority per card,
+  Rites (3, or 4 for an Invoker), Locks (1, or 2 for a Marksman), Contingencies (1, or 2 for a
+  Strategist), Gestalt upkeep at end of Turn, Psionic Strain with a free Surge and Breach
+  overflow, and Full Manifestation with a Rank cost table and one shared five-space
+  Manifestation Wound track.
+- Equipment terminology: a melee or Guard Fitting is a "Handling"; a shield is a "Guard item".
